  No, a bullet does not experience blunt impact after
  radial expansion; rather, the initial physical resistance
  (blunt impact/deceleration) happens at the exact
  same micro-instant contact and triggers the radial
  expansion (mushrooming) simultaneously.
  Here is how terminal ballistics actually work upon
  contact:
  • Initial Contact: The bullet nose strikes the target
  medium, creating an instantaneous physical
  resistance and deceleration shock.
  • Simultaneous Deformation: The pressure from
  this impact forces soft lead or hollow cavity
  structures backward, causing the outer edges to
  flare outward radially at the same time the kinetic
  energy transfer begins.
  • Energy Transfer: The radial widening creates a
  larger wound channel and brakes the projectile,
  converting the forward momentum into a wider
  energy spread inside the target.
  National Insti... +1
  If you are looking into specific terminal effects or
  testing methods like ballistic gelatin, let me know if
  you'd like to discuss how different designs-like
  Ask anything
  O
